add('P206-0001','diagram chase explicitly suppressed','Proposition 5.4.1: transformed exact sequence','The two closed-open long exact sequences, together with the comparison isomorphism on the complement, yield the displayed transformed sequence.','Write the morphism of long exact sequences, use the complement isomorphism to construct each connecting arrow, and prove independence of lifts and exactness by a diagram chase or a mapping cone. Verify the closed-subspace identification for equivariant cohomology.','fixed locus|closed-open sequence|mapping cone')
add('P206-0002','corollary with empty-locus qualification','Corollary to Proposition 5.4.1','For trivial action on the fixed locus and on field coefficients, the group/space decomposition gives the displayed exact sequence and direct-sum formula.','If X_0 is nonempty, condition (D) plus trivial action on X_0 forces G to be finite; if X_0 is empty that inference is false, although the terms supported there vanish. Apply the field Kunneth calculation only in the stated finite-group/trivial-action scope.','empty fixed locus|finite group|Kunneth')
add('P206-0003','Smith-theory application stated without proof','§5.4, Remarks: fixed points of a homology sphere','The Smith-type fixed-point conclusion is asserted as an application of the transformed sequences.','Specify the cohomology theory and finite-dimensional hypotheses, allow the appropriate empty-sphere convention, compute cyclic prime-order group cohomology, and analyze the spectral sequence and exact sequence until the fixed-set cohomology is determined. The terse statement is not a proof for arbitrary topological spaces.','Smith theory|fixed point|homology sphere')
add('P207-0001','further applications and research directions unproved','§5.4, Remarks: acyclic quotients and solvable groups','The text asserts acyclicity results for prime-order and finite solvable actions, extensions of coefficients, and possible applications to Steenrod operations and symmetric powers.','For the precise acyclicity hypotheses, carry out the prime-order Smith calculation, use averaging in characteristic different from the prime, justify passage from all fields to integer coefficients by the relevant universal-coefficient argument, and induct through a normal series. Steenrod and symmetric-power comments are proposed applications, not established theorems here.','Smith theory|solvable group|Steenrod|symmetric power')
add('P208-0001','equivariant cochain construction omitted','§5.5: G-covers and their cochain complexes','A G-cover and an equivariant presheaf induce a G-action on the Cech complex commuting with its differential.','Write the action by transporting the section and replacing every index with its inverse translate; verify the restriction maps and alternating differential are equivariant and the group law holds.','G-cover|Cech complex|group action')
add('P209-0001','closed-cover resolution and comparison sketched','Theorem 5.5.3, Corollary 2: closed covers','The finite-closed and locally-finite-paracompact closed-cover cases give the indicated spectral sequence.','For a finite closed cover, take stalks of the sheaf Cech complex and contract the simplex on the members containing the point; use finite products and closed-embedding cohomology. For a locally finite cover use local finiteness to retain this local contraction and a soft/acyclic comparison under paracompactness. Arbitrary closed covers do not have the stated descent property.','finite closed cover|locally finite|Cech resolution')
add('P211-0001','printed comparison direction requires reversal','§5.5, Remark 1(b): comparison of equivariant spectral sequences','The universal repeated cover refines toward an arbitrary free G-cover, giving the canonical comparison from the global group/space spectral sequence to the cover spectral sequence in the corrected direction.','Construct the equivariant refinement and the resulting filtered double-complex map; check its E2 map and abutment. The trivial-group circle good-cover test in the events rules out the reversed filtered comparison asserted in print.','spectral sequence comparison|G-cover|refinement direction')
add('P211-0002','equivariant prism homotopy omitted','§5.5, equation (5.5.12): independence of refinement map','Two equivariant refinement maps induce chain-homotopic maps on Cech complexes and hence the same hypercohomology map.','Write the signed prism sum inserting the two index maps, check ds+sd equals their difference, and note every summand is equivariant. For general refinements replace both index sets by free G-indexed covers before choosing equivariant maps.','equivariant prism|refinement homotopy|Cech')
add('P212-0001','printed free-cover implication is false','§5.5: construction of general refinement maps','A free index action on the coarser cover alone does not ensure an equivariant map from the finer index set.','The point with group C2, free coarser indices and a fixed singleton finer index is a counterexample. Use free replacements of both covers, choose orbit representatives on the finer free cover, and prove independence and transitivity through equivariant prism homotopies. This repairs the construction rather than refuting the resulting refinement-invariance theorem.','free refinement|equivariant map|C2')
add('P212-0002','local-zero cochain killing argument needs care','Lemma 5.5.5: reduction to local annihilation','Under (D) and paracompactness of the quotient, the proof seeks a refined free G-cover killing a given locally zero equivariant presheaf cochain.','Construct the slice covers and show that only finitely many relevant group tuples occur near a fixed quotient point. The remaining annihilation must be arranged on actual refined intersections; local zero germs do not for an arbitrary presheaf imply zero on one neighborhood of a finite fibre taken as a whole.','locally zero presheaf|equivariant refinement|paracompact quotient')
add('P213-0001','finite-fibre step is false for arbitrary presheaves','Lemma 5.5.5: finite-fibre neighborhood step','The asserted passage from germwise zero to zero on a neighborhood of a finite fibre requires an extra argument or a finite-additivity hypothesis on the presheaf.','The recorded two-point free C2 presheaf with P(X)=Z and zero values on singletons has zero sheafification but a nonzero section over the entire fibre. A pointwise refinement can kill it; to complete the lemma use a compatible refinement of intersections or first pass through a justified additive replacement. The counterexample refutes the proof step, not the lemma’s conclusion.','finite fibre|presheaf sheafification|refinement gap')
add('P213-0002','geometric interpretation and proposed theory unproved','§5.5: equivariant torsors and nonabelian extension','Equivariant Cech H^1 classifies equivariant torsors; the text proposes a broader noncommutative homological algebra.','Glue trivial torsors from cocycles and show equivariant transition data are exactly the cocycle conditions, then identify changes of trivialization with coboundaries. For abelian coefficients the low-degree comparison follows from the spectral-sequence edge analysis. A general nonabelian homological-algebra framework is a programme direction, not a theorem proved here.','equivariant torsor|nonabelian cohomology|gerbe')
add('P214-0001','locality and bifunctor verification omitted','§5.6: equivariant Ext','The derived invariant Hom constructions are cohomological in both arguments and have the stated local sheafification descriptions.','Use enough injectives in the equivariant module category, preservation of injectivity under restriction to invariant opens, and exactness of contravariant Hom into an injective. The sheaf-level calculation requires the corrected stabilizer and product conventions in the following lemma.','equivariant Ext|internal Hom|bifunctor')
